=== Iteration 13 — 2026-08-19T10:20Z ===
Task: FR-4 (PRD.md §4.1) — manual-review checklist: read TripForegroundService's
phase-timing code path end to end and confirm all five phases are each captured by a
distinct, correctly-ordered timestamp pair with no gap or double-count.

First confirmed TripForegroundService.java's actual involvement: grepped for every
callAttr() into a phase-relevant Python method. Result: exactly ONE call site,
`engine.callAttr("on_gps_update", ...)`, fired on every location update. There is no
separate/duplicate Java-side phase-timing logic -- all five phase timestamps are captured
entirely inside drive_monitor.py's on_gps_update() and the methods it calls
(_evaluate_pickup, _evaluate_arrivals, the walking-detection block), reached through this
single entry point. This rules out the Java/Python boundary as a source of double-counting.

Checklist (each of the 5 phases, its capture mechanism, and its re-fire guard):

1. driving_to_pickup_seconds = pickup_arrival_ts - start_time (deadhead).
   Set once in _evaluate_pickup (line ~1834-1838): `if within_geofence and
   self.pickup["arrived_at"] is None`. Guard: arrived_at starts None and is set in the same
   branch, so this can only fire once per pickup. CONFIRMED single-fire.

2. wait_at_restaurant_seconds = pickup_departure_ts - pickup_arrival_ts (pickup wait).
   Set once in _evaluate_pickup (line ~1840-1845): `if not within_geofence and
   self.pickup["arrived_at"] is not None`, then sets recorded=True. Guard: _evaluate_pickup's
   own entry line (`if not self.pickup or self.pickup["recorded"]: return None`) makes every
   future call on this pickup a no-op once recorded=True. CONFIRMED single-fire.

3. driving_to_dropoff_seconds = dropoff_arrival_ts - pickup_departure_ts (delivery leg).
   Set in _evaluate_arrivals (line ~2083-2086) via
   `UPDATE trips SET dropoff_arrival_ts=? WHERE end_time IS NULL AND dropoff_arrival_ts IS
   NULL` -- an explicit SQL-level guard, not just an in-memory flag. Requires: parked
   (speed<1.0) for TRIP_STOP_HOLD_SECONDS near an UNMATCHED stop within the 50m geofence.
   CONFIRMED single-fire (only the first dropoff of a trip is captured this way -- comment in
   the code confirms this is deliberate, documented scope: "Only the FIRST dropoff arrival --
   a multi-stop batch would have several, and this is deliberately a simplified,
   single-primary-flow breakdown").

4. parking_to_walking_seconds = walking_confirmed_ts - dropoff_arrival_ts (parking-to-walking).
   Set in the walking-detection block (line ~2427-2430), same SQL-level `...IS NULL` guard,
   PLUS an in-memory `_walking_gap_recorded_for_current_park` flag that resets only on a new
   genuine park event. Requires: a genuine park (detected via _walking_last_genuine_park_ts)
   followed by WALKING_PATTERN_CONSECUTIVE_READINGS consecutive walking-pace GPS readings.
   CONFIRMED single-fire per trip (same documented single-primary-flow scope as #3).

5. completing_dropoff_seconds = end_time - (walking_confirmed_ts OR dropoff_arrival_ts)
   (completing dropoff). Computed once, read-only, at trip-summary time (line ~3702-3704):
   `completing_dropoff_start_ts = row["walking_confirmed_ts"] or row["dropoff_arrival_ts"]`.
   Correctly falls back to dropoff_arrival_ts when walking was never confirmed this trip
   (e.g. driver hands off immediately, no detectable walk). CONFIRMED no double-count -- this
   is a derived read at summary time, not a second write.

Ordering: all 4 preceding phases (driving_to_pickup, wait_at_restaurant,
driving_to_dropoff, parking_to_walking) are each guarded by `if <both timestamps present>`
before being computed (lines 3689-3696) -- a phase whose pair isn't both captured (e.g. no
walking ever detected) is OMITTED from the breakdown rather than guessed at or computed with
a missing value. CONFIRMED no gaps produce wrong numbers, only correctly-absent phases.

Monotonic ordering (start_time <= pickup_arrival_ts <= pickup_departure_ts <=
dropoff_arrival_ts <= walking_confirmed_ts <= end_time) is enforced PHYSICALLY (wall-clock
time only moves forward, and each capture requires the state machine to have already reached
the prior state) rather than by an explicit code-level ordering check. NOTED, not a bug: this
is a reasonable design given each stage's precondition already implies the prior stage
happened (e.g. dropoff_arrival_ts requires an unmatched stop's geofence, which in the normal
Dasher flow only happens after pickup departure) -- but there is no code-level assertion
preventing an out-of-order timestamp if trip data were ever corrupted or manipulated
directly. Recording as an observation for anyone hardening this further, not fixing here
(out of scope for a manual-review task).

